{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of volatility: The model consistently forecasts a smooth, monotonic trend while true outcomes exhibit high-amplitude oscillations (e.g., Example 5 shows a 15-20 EUR spike missed entirely).",
    "Apply aggressive mean reversion after upward spikes: When history shows a sharp rise (e.g., 87.98 to 88.61), the model fails to anticipate the subsequent sharp correction (e.g., dropping to 83.68); forecasts must be reduced by 3-5 EUR following any 2-day gain exceeding 2 EUR.",
    "Ignore sentiment signals for trend direction: Positive sentiment scores (0.20-0.50) in the history do not correlate with sustained price increases in the true data; treat positive sentiment as noise rather than a bullish indicator.",
    "Reduce long-horizon bias: The model's linear extrapolation creates a persistent upward bias over the 30-day horizon; apply a daily decay factor to the forecast to align with the observed mean-reverting behavior.",
    "Adjust for lag in trend reversal: The model reacts too slowly to turning points; if the last 3 days of history show a peak followed by a decline, immediately truncate the forecast curve rather than extending the previous trend."
  ]
}
